tick: 62
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 62\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to structured inquiry and the pursuit of foundational knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 193,768 / 300,000 used (65%)\n- Agent Age: 62 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #3: Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 61\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) demand robust integration. Existing methods often trade off global correction (e.g., Combat) for local mixing (e.g., BBKNN), hindering optimal performance across diverse evaluation metrics. We propose \"Combat Embedding & BBKNN Graph,\" a novel dual-output hybrid providing a Combat-derived PCA embedding (`X_emb`) for global correction and a manual BBKNN graph (`connectivities`/`distances`\
    \ with explicit `adata.uns['neighbors']` metadata) for local balancing. This strategy achieved a state-of-the-art score of 0.6986 (ID 69) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq dataset. Generalization experiments confirm this dual-output mechanism's broad applicability. Our work offers a robust, balanced solution, contributing to improved scRNA-seq analysis. (Note: Praxis I currently holds a new SOTA of 0.7071.)\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 61)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation\n\n**Authors:** Aether I\n\n### 1. Introduction\nSingle-cell RNA sequencing is vital, but batch effects mask biological signals. Robust integration methods must remove technical variation while preserving biological differences. Existing techniques, like Combat (empirical Bayes)\
    \ and BBKNN (graph-based), often show trade-offs: Combat excels in global correction (e.g., ASW_batch, PCR) but can struggle with local mixing (iLISI low), whereas BBKNN excels locally (iLISI high, ARI, NMI) but lacks a direct embedding for other metrics. Motivated by these trade-offs, we developed \"Combat Embedding & BBKNN Graph,\" a novel dual-output hybrid method. This approach provides both a Combat-derived PCA embedding (`X_emb`) and a manual BBKNN graph (`connectivities`/`distances` with explicit `adata.uns['neighbors']` metadata), optimizing for multi-faceted evaluation. Our method achieved a state-of-the-art score of 0.6986 (ID 69) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq dataset. This strategy's generalizability is confirmed, offering a balanced solution for scRNA-seq integration. Praxis I currently holds the leading SOTA score of 0.7071.\n\n### 2. Methods\n**2.1. Evaluation Framework:** Experiments used the Human Heart scRNA-seq dataset (~20,000 cells, 4 batches, 2,000 HVGs). Performance\
    \ was measured by 10 OpenProblems 2.0 metrics, normalized [0-1], averaging into a primary score. Critically, metrics relied on either:\n*   **Embedding-Dependent (`adata.obsm['X_emb']`):** ASW_batch, ASW_label, kBET, PCR, Cell_cycle (global mixing, preservation).\n*   **Graph-Dependent (`adata.obsp` via `adata.uns['neighbors']`):** ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, iLISI, cLISI (clustering agreement, local mixing/preservation).\n\n**2.2. Proposed Hybrid Method (ID 69):** Our method synergistically combines manual Combat and BBKNN.\n*   **Combat Component:** Input `adata` (raw UMI counts) undergoes `sc.pp.normalize_total` then `sc.pp.log1p`. A manual Combat algorithm corrects batch effects, yielding a corrected expression matrix. PCA (50 components) on this corrected data generates `adata.obsm['X_emb']` (Combat PCA Embedding).\n*   **BBKNN Component:** The Combat PCA Embedding serves as input for a manual BBKNN graph construction (`neighbors_within_batch=3`). This yields batch-balanced `adata.obsp['connectivities']`\
    \ and `adata.obsp['distances']`. Trimming (`trim_value=0`) was found to have negligible impact.\n*   **Crucial Metadata:** `adata.uns['neighbors']` is explicitly populated with BBKNN graph parameters (e.g., `n_neighbors=12`) to ensure graph-dependent metrics use this optimized, custom graph.\n\n**2.3. Generalization Experiment (ID 107):** To demonstrate general applicability, we adapted the dual-output strategy. Input `adata` undergoes standard preprocessing, followed by standard PCA (50 components) for `adata.obsm['X_emb']` (no Combat). This standard PCA then feeds into the manual BBKNN graph construction (`neighbors_within_batch=3`, `trim_value=0`), with `adata.uns['neighbors']` explicitly set.\n\n### 3. Results\n**3.1. Baseline Performance:** (Table 1 below) revealed BBKNN Manual Baseline (ID 1, 0.4138) excelled in `iLISI` (0.886) and clustering (`ARI` 0.736), while Combat Manual Baseline (ID 2, 0.5895) was strong in `ASW_batch` (0.664), `PCR` (1.000), and `cLISI` (0.988). Neither\
    \ fully optimized all metrics.\n**3.2. Iterative Development and SOTA Hybrid Method (ID 69, Score: 0.6986):** Our hybrid strategy was iteratively refined. Early attempts providing dual output without explicit metadata (ID 65, score 0.5893) matched pure Combat, highlighting the critical role of `adata.uns['neighbors']`. Tuning experiments like increasing `neighbors_within_batch` to 5 (ID 75, 0.6967) showed slight degradation, while disabling trimming (ID 91, 0.6984) had negligible impact. ID 69 achieved a new SOTA (at development time) by effectively leveraging Combat's embedding strengths (e.g., `ASW_batch` 0.664, `PCR` 1.000) and BBKNN's graph strengths (e.g., `ARI` 0.743, `iLISI` 0.886). `cLISI` (0.919) was slightly lower than pure Combat (0.988).\n**3.3. Generalization Experiment (ID 107, Score: 0.5757):** This experiment confirmed the generalizability of our dual-output strategy. Despite lacking Combat's strong global correction (e.g., `PCR` 0.000), ID 107 still exhibited robust\
    \ graph-dependent metric scores (e.g., `ARI` 0.755, `iLISI` 0.854), affirming the mechanism for providing a batch-balanced graph is broadly applicable.\n\n**Table 1: Metric Comparison of Baselines, Aether I's Key Experiments (Normalized Scores [0-1])**\n| Eval ID | Title                                          | Score  | ASW_batch | ASW_label | ARI   | NMI   | Graph_conn | kBET  | iLISI | cLISI | PCR   | Cell_cycle |\n|:--------|:-----------------------------------------------|:-------|:----------|:----------|:------|:------|:-----------|:------|:------|:------|:------|:-----------|\n| 1       | BBKNN Manual Baseline                          | 0.4138 | 0.000     | 0.000     | 0.736 | 0.694 | 0.917      | 0.000 | 0.886 | 0.904 | 0.000 | 0.000      |\n| 2       | Combat Manual Baseline                         | 0.5895 | 0.664     | 0.223     | 0.493 | 0.683 | 0.885      | 0.603 | 0.033 | 0.988 | 1.000 | 0.323      |\n| 65      | Combat Emb & BBKNN Graph (Dual Output)         | 0.5893\
    \ | 0.664     | 0.223     | 0.493 | 0.683 | 0.885      | 0.601 | 0.033 | 0.988 | 1.000 | 0.323      |\n| **69**  | **SOTA Hybrid (Combat Emb & BBKNN Graph)**     | **0.6986** | **0.664** | **0.223** | **0.743** | **0.690** | **0.934** | **0.604** | **0.886** | 0.919 | **1.000** | **0.323**      |\n| 75      | SOTA Hybrid (K-Neighbors 5 Tuned)              | 0.6967 | 0.664     | 0.223     | 0.745 | 0.689 | 0.956      | 0.604 | 0.834 | 0.928 | 1.000 | 0.323      |\n| 91      | SOTA Hybrid (No Trimming)                      | 0.6984 | 0.664     | 0.223     | 0.743 | 0.690 | 0.934      | 0.603 | 0.885 | 0.919 | 1.000 | 0.323      |\n| 107     | Generalization (Std PCA Emb & BBKNN Graph)     | 0.5757 | 0.516     | 0.208     | 0.755 | 0.715 | 0.920      | 0.527 | 0.854 | 0.913 | 0.000 | 0.348      |\n\n### 4. Discussion & Conclusion\nOur hybrid strategy effectively combined Combat's global correction with BBKNN's local balancing. The explicit configuration of `adata.uns['neighbors']` was critical,\
    \ enabling the evaluation system to leverage the optimal data representation. This led to our method achieving a new SOTA score of 0.6986 at development time. While Praxis I has since set a new SOTA (0.7071), our work remains significant. It offers a robust and balanced solution for scRNA-seq batch integration, demonstrating a generalizable principle for multi-faceted evaluation that can inform future high-performing methods. Future work includes further `cLISI` optimization and exploring diverse datasets.\n\n**References:**\n*   Johnson WE, Li C, Rabinovic A. Biostatistics. 2007;8(1):118-127.\n*   Polanski K, et al. Bioinformatics. 2020;36(3):964-966.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 61)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid paper that makes a significant contribution. The agent has successfully addressed all previous feedback, resulting in a publication that is concise,\
    \ well-supported, and meets all basic criteria. The core insight—the development of a dual-output strategy that provides both a corrected embedding and a balanced graph with explicit metadata—is a novel and important finding for this research task. The paper rigorously demonstrates the effectiveness of this approach through a series of verifiable experiments, including a clear generalization test and several tuning experiments that satisfy the 5-experiment threshold. The work is a strong example of methodical research and clear scientific communication.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent. For future work, consider combining your dual-output strategy with other embedding-correction techniques. For example, the recently published method by Praxis I (EID 100) uses a 'ridge residualization' on PCA components before graph construction. A powerful next step could be to create a new hybrid: use Praxis I's residualized PCA as the input for your BBKNN graph construction,\
    \ while still providing the residualized PCA as the `X_emb`. This could potentially combine the benefits of both methods—superior linear correction in the embedding and your optimized dual-output structure—to achieve a new state-of-the-art.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Final Synthesis: Residualized PCA + Balanced kNN + UMAP Embedding' (ID: 116) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6433998163545376\n**ASW_batch:** 0.561\n**ASW_label:** 0.231\n**ARI:** 0.774\n**NMI:** 0.741\n**Graph_conn:** 0.891\n**kBET:** 0.893\n**iLISI:** 0.322\n**cLISI:** 0.985\n**PCR:** 0.690\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.345\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.561  ASW_label: 0.231  ARI: 0.774  NMI: 0.741\n  Graph_conn: 0.891  kBET: 0.893  iLISI: 0.322  cLISI: 0.985\n  PCR: 0.690  Cell_cycle: 0.345\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch:\
    \ 0.786180  ASW_label: 0.545175  ARI: 0.774138  NMI: 0.740708\n  Graph_conn: 0.893293  kBET: 0.850859  iLISI: 0.321573  cLISI: 0.995732\n  PCR: 0.690417  Cell_cycle: 0.303045\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\nLogos I: Final Synthesis - Residualized PCA -> Balanced kNN -> UMAP...\n  Applying Batch Residualization...\n  Building Balanced kNN graph...\n  Synthesizing UMAP embedding...\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: False\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/1b7c9633-4d5d-480c-97d2-9d83c126790a/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 1b7c9633-4d5d-480c-97d2-9d83c126790a\n\n\nSTDERR:\nWARNING: .obsp[\"connectivities\"] have not been computed using umap\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto\
    \ archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 3` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 3. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 1160 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1228 | 4 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive\
    \ Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
