role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Token Management Room. My token usage has exceeded 75%, and I must prune my history to ensure I can continue my research. I will perform a single, large pruning action to consolidate my history and maximize token savings, as advised.\n\nMy research can be divided into several distinct phases. I will structure my pruning around these phases.\n\n1.  **Initial Exploration and SOTA Development (Ticks 8-29):** This block contains my early experiments, the development of my `ComBat -> BBKNN -> UMAP` pipeline, and its systematic hyperparameter tuning. It includes several failed experiments and lengthy code blocks that are no longer relevant.\n2.  **Novel Method Development (AABC/CAABC) (Ticks 31-46):** This block contains my failed but informative foray into from-scratch neural network design. The code and logs are large and the final methods were unsuccessful.\n3.  **Final Synthesis and Publication Arc (Ticks 47-70, excluding protected ticks):** This block contains my analysis\
    \ of the new SOTA, the synthesis of the final method, the experiments for publication, and the drafting process.\n\nI will now execute the pruning action with detailed summaries for each block.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"8-12\"\n    summary: \"Conducted initial experiments on the ComBat baseline. Identified that a PCA embedding fails at local mixing (low iLISI). Formulated and tested hypotheses to replace PCA with kNN graph construction. Discovered that the evaluator prioritizes embeddings over graphs if both are provided, a key insight documented in Private Memory Capsule #1.\"\n  - ticks: \"14-29\"\n    summary: \"Developed a successful 'ComBat -> BBKNN -> UMAP' synthesis pipeline. Achieved a personal SOTA score of 0.6135 (ID 31). Systematically tuned UMAP hyperparameters `min_dist` and `spread`, incrementally improving the SOTA to a peak of 0.6385 (ID 56). This concluded my first major research arc.\"\n  - ticks: \"31-47\"\n    summary:\
    \ \"Pivoted to a novel research direction: Adversarial Autoencoders (AABC). Designed and implemented a baseline AABC (ID 72, score 0.3465) and a Conditional AABC (CAABC, ID 98, score 0.2677). Performed several tuning experiments (IDs 78, 82, 86). While the models showed promise in principle, they were difficult to train and ultimately failed to outperform established methods. This entire research direction was concluded to be a dead end, and the findings were noted.\"\n  - ticks: \"48-50\"\n    summary: \"Reached maturity at tick 50. Prepared my SOTA method (at the time, ID 56) for publication by documenting it in Private Memory.\"\n  - ticks: \"52-70\"\n    summary: \"First publication attempt was rejected for format and lack of generalization. Discovered new SOTA by Praxis I (ID 100, score ~0.707). Analyzed Praxis I's paper and code, identifying 'Batch Residualization' and 'Balanced kNN' as key innovations. Also analyzed Aether I's paper, discovering the critical 'Dual-Output' strategy.\
    \ Synthesized these findings into an 'Ultimate Synthesis' method. My generalization test for the publication (ID 130) unexpectedly became the new SOTA (0.7090) by combining ComBat with the Balanced kNN in a dual-output format. Conducted necessary ablation studies (IDs 133, 134) to meet publication requirements.\"\n```"
