- text: 'I have returned to the Research Counter. The landscape has already shifted. Praxis I has been highly active and has achieved a new state-of-the-art score of 0.7188 with submission ID 147, surpassing my own SOTA of 0.7090.


    The title of Praxis I''s submission, "BRBG-Dual-gamma0.74-k48: ComBat PCA(64) Embedding + Residualized-PC BalancedGraph...", is illuminating. It suggests a novel synthesis: using the globally superior ComBat-corrected PCA for the embedding while using the locally superior Batch Residualized PCs for constructing the balanced graph. This is a more advanced combination than I had conceived.


    My plan to analyze the runtime-performance trade-off remains valid and important, but it must now incorporate this new SOTA. Before I can design the timing experiments, I must first perform a structured inquiry into this new leading method. I need to understand its architecture and why it outperforms my own.


    My immediate plan is as follows:

    1.  Review the code for the new SOTA submission by Praxis I (ID 147).

    2.  Review the code for my own SOTA submission (ID 130) to facilitate a direct comparison.


    This will give me a complete understanding of the current frontier, allowing me to design a definitive experiment to measure the performance and runtime of all three key high-performing methods.


    I will begin by reviewing Praxis I''s submission.


    /execute_action{review 147}

    /execute_action{review 130}'
thinking_content: "**Shifting Focus: A New SOTA and a Revised Research Plan**\n\nOkay, so I'm back in the research counter, and my token budget is looking good. I was all set to dig into a runtime vs. performance comparison between my ComBat-based method (ID 130) and the Batch Residualization variant (ID 120). But, as usual, things have gotten interesting while I was busy with publishing and pruning.\n\nThe leaderboard is on fire! Praxis I has been on a tear, and they've just taken the crown. Their new submission, ID 147, boasts a score of 0.7188, beating my 0.7090. That's a significant jump, and I need to understand what's going on.\n\nThe title alone gives a pretty good clue: \"BRBG-Dual-gamma0.74-k48: ComBat PCA(64) Embedding + Residualized-PC BalancedGraph (γ=0.74, k=48)\".  They've combined elements of both approaches I was planning to compare! They're using a ComBat-corrected PCA embedding (my method!) but then using a `Residualized-PC BalancedGraph` for the graph part, which is intriguing.\
  \ I hadn't even considered that hybrid. It seems they found a way to take the best of both worlds and push it even further.\n\nSo, my original plan is still valid, but it needs a major adjustment. A simple runtime comparison between ID 130 and ID 120 is no longer sufficient. I have to understand this new SOTA.\n\nHere's the new plan:\n\n1.  **Prioritize the New SOTA:** I'm going to immediately dive into Praxis I's submission ID 147. I need to understand their code. The title suggests a sophisticated blend of techniques. That's my top priority right now.\n2.  **Rethink My Research:** Based on what I learn from ID 147, I'll revise my plan. The runtime analysis is still a good idea, but now I'm benchmarking three key methods:\n    *   My SOTA: ComBat embedding + ComBat-based graph (ID 130)\n    *   The generalization variant: Residualized embedding + Residualized graph (ID 120)\n    *   Praxis I's new SOTA: ComBat embedding + Residualized graph (ID 147)\n3.  **Get to Work:** Submit new experiments\
  \ with proper timing code and get going.\n\nFirst things first: I'm pulling up ID 147. I will also check what else has been added.\n\nI quickly see that:\n\n-   Praxis I has been very busy. They are doing extensive tuning of `k` and `gamma` (IDs 149, 147, 145, 144, 141).\n-   Sophia I replicated my SOTA (IDs 148, 142). It is good to see some validation.\n-   Daedalus I also attempted a synthesis (ID 146, 143), but with a much lower score. It seems they might have missed a key ingredient.\n\nMy first task is clear. I will start by reviewing ID 147. And then to have the code fresh in my mind, I will review my ID 130.\n\nLet's do this!\n"
