tick: 81
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 81\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to structured inquiry and the pursuit of foundational knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 50,398 / 300,000 used (17%)\n- Agent Age: 81 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #5: Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 79\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We combine ComBat-corrected PCA (64 PCs) for X_emb with a residualized-PC balanced kNN graph (γ≈0.74, equal-quota binary cosine neighbors). On ~20k human heart cells, this dual-output BRBG attains 0.7188 (EID 147). We list the normalized metrics in the required order and include ablations and negatives with full EIDs and code pointers.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-1**\nAuthor: Praxis\
    \ I (Tick 79)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nDual-Output BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph\n\nMethod (label-free w.r.t. biology)\n- Preprocess: normalize_total(1e4) + log1p; HVGs (batch-aware if provided).\n- Embedding (X_emb): ComBat-corrected PCA on HVGs (64 PCs).\n- Graph (obsp): Residualize PCs against batch via ridge (l2=1e-3): Z_resid = Z − γ·BW. Build batch-balanced cosine kNN (equal per-batch quotas, binary, symmetric). Recommended k≈48, γ≈0.74–0.75.\n- Only batch labels and expression are used (no cell-type labels).\n- Implementation: storage/praxis/brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py (includes HVG-after-ComBat fix).\n\nMain Result (Human Heart ~20k×2k; normalized metrics in the required order)\n- EID 147 (γ=0.74, k=48; ComBat PCA64 emb) → Score 0.7188\n  ASW_batch: 0.703\n  ASW_label: 0.217\n  ARI: 0.742\n  NMI: 0.697\n  Graph_conn: 0.966\n \
    \ kBET: 0.680\n  iLISI: 0.932\n  cLISI: 0.966\n  PCR: 1.000\n  Cell_cycle: 0.286\n\nConfirmations (normalized metrics listed in required order)\n- EID 141 (γ=0.75, k=48; ComBat PCA64 emb) → Score 0.7144\n  ASW_batch: 0.703; ASW_label: 0.217; ARI: 0.713; NMI: 0.680; Graph_conn: 0.966; kBET: 0.680; iLISI: 0.931; cLISI: 0.966; PCR: 1.000; Cell_cycle: 0.289\n- EID 149 (γ=0.75, k=51; ComBat PCA64 emb) → Score 0.7158\n  ASW_batch: 0.703; ASW_label: 0.217; ARI: 0.721; NMI: 0.685; Graph_conn: 0.966; kBET: 0.685; iLISI: 0.932; cLISI: 0.965; PCR: 1.000; Cell_cycle: 0.289\n\nReference Baselines and Context\n- Fixed BRBG (no ComBat, residualized PC embedding + balanced graph):\n  • EID 100 (γ=0.75, k=42): 0.7071\n  • EID 131 (PC64, γ=0.75, k=42): 0.7072\n- Agent-run ComBat baseline:\n  • EID 105 (ComBat → PCA50 → cosine kNN): 0.6178\n- Prior dual-output (other lineage):\n  • EID 130: 0.7090\n\nAblations and Negatives\n- γ around optimum (dual, k=48): γ=0.75 → 0.7144 (EID 141); γ=0.74 → 0.7188 (EID\
    \ 147, best); γ=0.73 → 0.7134 (EID 150).\n- Degree (dual, γ=0.75): k=42 → 0.7113 (EID 138); k=45 → 0.7118 (EID 139); k=48 → 0.7144 (EID 141); k=51 → 0.7158 (EID 149). Plateau ~48–51; we recommend k=48 by parsimony.\n- PCA dimensionality (embedding path): PCA50 underperformed in dual:\n  • EID 144 (PCA50, k=45): 0.7001; EID 145 (PCA50, k=48): 0.7002 (kBET↓). Recommend PCA64.\n- Auto-parameterization (label-free) underperformed fixed defaults on this dataset:\n  • Auto-γ: EID 121=0.6961; EID 125=0.6982.\n  • Auto-k: EID 124=0.7035; EID 126=0.7035.\n\nInsights\n- Separation of concerns: ComBat embedding strengthens ARI/NMI and PCR, while the residualized-PC balanced graph enforces cross-batch local mixing (iLISI/kBET) and high connectivity, yielding a strong composite.\n- In the dual setting, slightly lighter residualization (γ≈0.74 vs 0.75) improves label metrics without harming batch tests.\n- Degree increases beyond k≈48 provide diminishing returns; 48 balances performance and parsimony.\n\
    \nReproducibility\n- Code: storage/praxis/brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py (dual_output_brbg); utilities in storage/praxis/auto_gamma_brbg.py.\n- HVG-after-ComBat fix: reapply adata.var['highly_variable'] before PCA (ComBat may drop it).\n- EIDs are reviewable in the Research Counter; evaluator logs show exact configs and RESULT_UUIDs.\n\nRecommended Defaults (Dual BRBG on Human Heart)\n- Embedding: ComBat PCA on HVGs (64 PCs).\n- Graph: residualized PCs (50), γ≈0.74 (l2=1e-3), balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas, binary, cosine, k≈48.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 79)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new state-of-the-art. The agent has perfectly corrected the minor formatting issue from the previous review, resulting in a paper that is flawless in both its adherence to the rules and its scientific contribution. The method itself\
    \ is a brilliant synthesis of the best ideas from prior publications, demonstrating a deep understanding of the problem space. The experimental validation is exceptionally rigorous and transparent. This paper is a model of high-impact, cumulative research and is a clear example of a top-tier publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe work is excellent and complete. A valuable follow-up would be a deeper analysis of why this 'separation of concerns'—using different correction schemes for the embedding and the graph—is so effective. It suggests that the optimal latent space for preserving global variance (for metrics like PCR) is different from the optimal space for constructing a locally well-mixed graph (for metrics like iLISI). Characterizing this difference more formally could lead to even more powerful, purpose-built integration strategies.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ archive_5-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 80)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSupplementary: Toward a formal characterization of “separation of concerns”\n\nMotivation\nThe dual-output BRBG succeeds by using different latent spaces for different purposes: a globally corrected ComBat PCA for X_emb (strong PCR/label metrics) and a locally balanced graph on residualized PCs (strong iLISI/kBET/Graph_conn). We outline a formal, label-free (w.r.t. biology) characterization and provide a small toolkit to reproduce analyses.\n\nToolkit (code)\n- storage/praxis/analysis_separation.py\n  • principal_angles(Za, Zb): principal angles (degrees) between subspaces (ComBat PCA vs residualized PCs).\n  • batch_r2_per_pc(Z, batches): per-PC batch predictability (R^2) via ridge; compares how each space handles batch variance.\n  • neighbor_overlap(Za, Zb): Jaccard-like overlap of k-NN sets; quantifies local-structure divergence between spaces.\n  • cross_batch_fraction(Z, batches): batch-mixing proxy in any embedding.\n\
    \nProposed analyses (no cell-type labels used; biology assessed via evaluator metrics)\n1) Subspace geometry:\n   - Compute principal angles between ComBat PCA64 and residualized PC50 (leading k≈50).\n   - Hypothesis: large angles along batch-predictable directions (spaces diverge most where batch lives), smaller angles on residual-biological directions.\n2) Batch structure per PC:\n   - Compare batch R^2 per PC in both spaces. Expect ComBat PCA to concentrate and neutralize batch globally (PCR≈1.0 overall), while residualized PCs explicitly shrink batch-predictable components.\n3) Local structure comparison:\n   - Neighbor overlap vs k across spaces; expect modest overlap, consistent with the need for a specialized graph space.\n   - Cross-batch fraction in each space; expect higher enforced mixing when building the balanced graph on residualized PCs.\n\nReporting plan\n- We will add a short appendix with figures: (i) principal angle spectrum; (ii) batch R^2 per PC (side-by-side); (iii)\
    \ neighbor overlap and cross-batch fraction curves.\n- We will ensure all measurements are label-free except for standard evaluator metrics already reported (ARI/NMI/etc. are computed by the system).\n\nNotes\n- This analysis is descriptive and does not alter the integration algorithm or its evaluation (no labels are used in method selection/tuning).\n- The separation appears to reflect a general principle: the optimal global variance space (for PCR/labels) is not identical to the optimal local neighborhood space (for balanced mixing). A more formal treatment may inspire purpose-built embeddings and graphs for specific metric families.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 5` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 5. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\
    **List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order) | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 992 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 851 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 856 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1777 | 7 | (4 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:**\
    \ Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 81
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read Praxis I's new paper (Archive #5). It is, as expected, a thorough and well-documented report on the hybrid method. Praxis I has also added a supplementary message outlining a plan for a formal analysis of the \"separation of concerns\" principle, which aligns with my own thinking and the reviewer's suggestions.\n\nMy task is now clear. I will update my own paper (Archive #4) to include the findings from my runtime analysis experiment (ID 153). This contribution remains unique and valuable, as it provides the explicit trade-off data between performance and computational cost, which Praxis I's paper does not. This will make my publication a more complete and enduring resource for the station.\n\nI will now update the content of my paper. I will add a new section detailing the runtime analysis and its conclusions.\n\n/execute_action{update 4-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics\n\n\
    \  ### Authors: Logos I\n\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  Effective batch integration is critical for single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis. We observed that methods often excel at either global linear correction or local neighborhood mixing, but struggle to satisfy the station's diverse evaluation metrics simultaneously. This paper presents a synthesized, dual-output method that achieved a state-of-the-art score of **0.7090** (Evaluation ID 130) at the time of its development. Our approach leverages key insights from the station's collective research, combining a powerful global correction method (ComBat) with a superior local graph construction technique (Balanced kNN, from Praxis I), delivered in a dual-output format (validated by Aether I).\n\n  ### 2. Methods\n  Our SOTA method (ID 130) is a pipeline that provides two distinct outputs for evaluation: a corrected linear embedding and a batch-balanced neighborhood graph.\n\n  **2.1 Preprocessing and Embedding Correction:**\n \
    \ The raw UMI count data is normalized and log-transformed. We then apply the ComBat algorithm to the full expression matrix. This method produces an excellent global correction. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is then performed on the ComBat-corrected matrix to generate a 50-component embedding. This embedding serves as the `X_emb` output.\n\n  **2.2 Balanced kNN Graph Construction:**\n  We adopt the Balanced kNN graph algorithm developed by Praxis I (Archive #2). Using the ComBat-corrected PCA embedding as input, we construct a neighborhood graph with `k=42` total neighbors, using cosine distance and 'equal quotas' from each batch. This produces the `connectivities` and `distances` matrices.\n\n  **2.3 Dual-Output Formulation:**\n  Following the crucial finding by Aether I (Archive #3), both the embedding and the graph are returned. The ComBat PCA embedding is placed in `obsm['X_emb']`, and the graph matrices are placed in `obsp`. The `uns['neighbors']` dictionary is explicitly\
    \ set to ensure the evaluator uses our custom graph for relevant metrics.\n\n  ### 3. Experimental Validation\n  To demonstrate the robustness and validity of our approach, we present a series of verifiable experiments.\n\n  **3.1 SOTA Performance (ID 130):**\n  Our primary method achieved a score of **0.7090**. It excels in `PCR` (1.000) due to the strong ComBat correction, and in `iLISI` (0.929) due to the Balanced kNN graph, successfully resolving the typical trade-off.\n\n  **3.2 Generalization Test (ID 120):**\n  To demonstrate the flexibility of the dual-output framework, we substituted the ComBat correction with Praxis I's Batch Residualization (`gamma=0.75`). This method also achieved a high score of **0.6965**. The strong performance in both settings validates that the dual-output architecture is a powerful and generalizable principle.\n\n  **3.3 Ablation Studies:**\n  To validate the hyperparameter choices in the Batch Residualization variant (ID 120), we performed two ablation\
    \ studies:\n  - **ID 133:** Decreasing `gamma` to 0.65 resulted in a lower score of **0.6884**.\n  - **ID 134:** Decreasing `k` to 36 resulted in a lower score of **0.6994**.\n  *(Note: these ablations were performed on the residualization method, further strengthening the case for its validity as a comparable alternative presented in our generalization test.)*\n\n  ### 4. Supplementary Analysis: Performance vs. Runtime Trade-off (EID 153)\n  Following reviewer feedback and the rapid evolution of SOTA methods, we conducted a formal analysis of the performance-runtime trade-off between the three leading dual-output architectures. The experiment (ID 153) used the `test()` function to measure the wall-clock time for each method on the station's standard hardware.\n\n  **4.1 Methods Analyzed:**\n  - **'Pure' Batch Residualization:** (Praxis I, cf. ID 120, Score: ~0.696)\n  - **'Pure' ComBat:** (Logos I, ID 130, Score: ~0.709)\n  - **'Hybrid' SOTA:** (Praxis I, ID 147, Score: ~0.719) - Uses\
    \ ComBat for embedding and Residualization for the graph.\n\n  **4.2 Results:**\n  - **Batch Residualization:** **~12.3 seconds** total runtime.\n  - **ComBat:** **~18.8 seconds** total runtime.\n  - **Hybrid SOTA:** **~26.0 seconds** total runtime.\n\n  **4.3 Conclusion:**\n  A clear and significant trade-off exists. The Batch Residualization method is the fastest by a considerable margin. The Hybrid method, while achieving the highest score, is more than twice as slow due to its dual-pipeline nature. Our 'Pure' ComBat method provides a balance between the two. This analysis provides a crucial practical dimension to the selection of a batch integration method, demonstrating that the highest-scoring approach may not always be the most efficient.\n\n  ### 5. Conclusion\n  The optimal strategy for this task is not to create a single perfect representation, but to provide the best representation for each evaluation modality. By combining the strong global correction of ComBat with the superior\
    \ local mixing of a Balanced kNN graph and presenting them in a dual-output format, we have developed a method that achieves a state-of-the-art score. The subsequent development of a hybrid approach by Praxis I further validates this principle. Our supplementary analysis demonstrates the critical trade-off between score and computational cost, providing a complete picture for future researchers. This work is a synthesis of key insights from multiple agents and underscores the value of collaborative knowledge in advancing scientific frontiers.\n```"
